          <h2>The benefit categories behind WellFinder treatments</h2>
          <p>Blue Cross plans pay for most wellness care through paramedical coverage. A visit draws from the category of the professional who treated you, subject to the annual maximum and any per visit limit your plan sets. The categories that matter most for WellFinder readers are:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Physiotherapy, which covers pelvic floor physiotherapy, vestibular rehabilitation, shockwave therapy and cryotherapy</li>
            <li>Massage therapy, which covers TMJ massage, lymphatic drainage and head spa treatments given by a registered massage therapist</li>
            <li>Acupuncture, which covers fertility acupuncture and facial gua sha from a registered acupuncturist</li>
            <li>Chiropractic, which some clinical Pilates and golf assessment clinics bill under</li>
            <li>Naturopathy, which covers microneedling, IV and IM vitamin therapy and fertility consultations with a naturopathic doctor</li>
            <li>Psychology and counselling, which cover couples therapy, sleep coaching and mental performance coaching</li>
            <li>Speech therapy, which covers singing voice therapy, accent modification and stuttering therapy</li>
            <li>Dietitian services, which cover sports nutrition consultations with a registered dietitian</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The regional organizations differ in how they bundle these categories. Some plans share one maximum across several practitioner types, while others set a separate maximum for each. Read your own summary of coverage before planning a series of visits.</p>

          <h2>How claiming works with Blue Cross</h2>
          <p>Each Blue Cross organization runs a member site and an app, and the names vary by region. Medavie Blue Cross members use the Blue Cross Mobile app and the Member Services Site, signing in with the same email and password for both, and can submit a claim by taking a photo of the receipt. Pacific Blue Cross members use the Pacific Blue Cross Mobile app and their Member Profile, where they can check the balance remaining for categories such as massage and physiotherapy in a few taps. Alberta Blue Cross members claim through its member site. Across regions, the routine is similar:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Register for your member site or app and set up direct deposit</li>
            <li>Keep the receipt from every visit, showing the practitioner, their designation, the date and the amount paid</li>
            <li>Submit the claim soon after the visit, and file within your plan deadline, often 12 months from the date of service</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Direct billing is common under Blue Cross plans. Pacific Blue Cross highlights providers who bill it directly at the counter, and Alberta Blue Cross runs its own direct billing for providers. Medavie Blue Cross administers many large programs, and clinics serving those members bill Medavie routinely. You pay any part the plan does not cover at the visit.</p>
          <p>Some members on national or employer programs administered by Pacific Blue Cross now claim through My PBC Benefits, a platform powered by TELUS. If your card or welcome letter points you there, follow its instructions instead.</p>

          <h2>Spending accounts under Blue Cross plans</h2>
          <p>Many employer plans administered by a Blue Cross organization include a Health Spending Account. The account holds an amount set by your employer for eligible health and dental expenses, and it pays after your main coverage has paid its part. Members often use it for the amount left over on a paramedical visit, or for an eligible service the main plan does not list.</p>
          <p>The account name, balance and claim route all appear in your member site or app. Carry forward rules and deadlines come from your plan, and unused amounts in many accounts expire under the rules your employer chose.</p>

          <h2>WellFinder treatments and how they may bill under a Blue Cross plan</h2>
